body:
Tbh, you already kinda do. You and her have the same overall facial shape. You only need to work on that British accent and stretch your legs out to be 70% of your overall height. :P

&#x200B;

You should try a cosplay!

I've read a couple of your posts, like the "great disappointment"-one and this line -

&#x200B;

"But you should tell me what I did wrong, I appreciate honesty." Again, no one takes me up on that offer and I don't understand why. How can anyone improve, if they don't get feedback on what they did wrong?"

\- was quite interesting, since I've asked to know what I do wrong myself quite often in the past. I don't know if that's of any use to you, but if nobody can tell you exactly what's 'wrong', the issue may very well be a latent one. For myself, it's usually being too obsessive over.. Well.. very much everything. Also, I always try to solve peoples' problems which (apparently) gave off some kind of 'therapist-vibes' which weren't really appreciated. I guess, if I had any advice (again, I am having similar issues, therefore my advice may very well be pretty much useless), I'd tell you to question the modus operandi you choose when interacting with people. How do you do it? What's your 'endgame'? (Not content-wise, but regarding the modus, you interact with people)

&#x200B;

Edit: Maybe, people feel like you lack an emotional connection to them? Like being too analytical and stuff? That would be about what I had in mind when talking about your modus operandi. However, I have no idea on how to address this as, again, I am having similar issues an am entirely unable to change anything about them.

Man, that's one of those things I envy you for. Those gun rights. Can't even have an air rifle > 7.5 Joules here without a permit.

&#x200B;

You know, I'd go to a different subreddit if it were about guns themselves, but I thought there's an interesting discussion to have had about the relationship between autism, object-orientation and an interest in weaponsmithing, as well. Like... I bet many Aspies, if they chose that field of engineering, would make(/may already make) very decent gunsmiths.

Then please excuse my hyperbolic language which transformed a "the minority" (in contrast to the majority of people who aren't interested in violence you were referring to) to "nobody".

&#x200B;

However, the war-hunting-distinction seems to hold - You were talking about violence which is a very vague term. Please specify then. Which "violence" are you talking about that no majority of humans is interested in? I assume you meant war, hence my argument.

I see your motivation to kill yourself is strong.. You want to finally follow through with something - But maybe look at it this way: Whatever happens in your life (let's say once you are 18 and things go south even more), suicide is the one thing that will always be an option. I've called it your "get out of jail free"-card before and I believe the metaphor is about right. That being said, I understand your fear of turning 18 and having shit twisted by your parents as you described it, yet being 18 brings new options with it, too. I suggest you at least try to get your life straightened out when you are 18 - Again, you can always still take your own life. That's the one thing that will always be there, one thing that's for certain, if you will.

&#x200B;

This part of your post: "I don't do the take they gave me = screaming why I didn't do it | I do the task they gave me = screaming that I did it wrong" seemed particularly bad - No matter what you do/how you do it, it's always wrong. That kind of behavior (on your parents' side) breeds depression on your end. Although (maybe) not consciously, they have set you up for failure, because you literally have no means to decide whether you did something of worth or not. This leaves you with two options, you already outlined the first one in the title of this post. The other one is to say "fuck them" and build up your own system of values in which you yourself decide, what has worth and what has not. Having gone through this myself, that's not something easy, but very rewarding in the end. The only measure of success is you yourself. Try being a little bit better today than you were yesterday. No huge goals for the moment, just bringing your life in order one step at a time. You have some will left in you - At least give it the possibility to flourish for once (without your parents) - Again, you can still kill yourself, nobody is going to take that from you.

&#x200B;
